  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler and less humid today; warmer and more\nhumid Sunday with isolated afternoon thunderstorms. \n\nHigh pressure will pass north of the area today and then off \nthe Mid Atlantic coast Sunday. It will be slightly cooler and\nless humid today on NNW winds. As a result, most areas should \nremain dry and seasonable today, although an inverted surface \ntrough may result in isolated showers along the Allegheny \nMountains. Southerly flow returns Sunday, bringing increasing \nheat and humidity. A passing shortwave trough may support a few \nafternoon showers and thunderstorms about anywhere.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Late summer heat returns next week.\n\nGuidance remains steadfast on late summer heat returning to end \nmeteorological summer (June 1-August 31) and begin meteorological \nautumn (September 1-November 30).\n\nA strong mid-level ridge will build across the southern CONUS and \ninto the Mid-Atlantic beginning on Sunday, and persist through at \nleast the middle of the week. Some guidance has been trending \ntowards having the ridge axis a bit further south and west, which \nwould lower heights a bit and introduce more chances for showers and \nthunderstorms at times in large scale NW flow aloft. This lends to a \nbit of spread in the temperature forecast, especially if any back-\ndoor surface cold fronts slide into the region by mid/late week.\n\nDespite the uncertainties, it remains likely that high temperatures \nwill reach the lower to middle 90s for much of the region for much \nof the upcoming week. Depending on smaller scale details, dew points \ncould end up a little higher or lower than currently progged which \nwill ultimately affect how high heat index values get. Values \napproaching Heat Advisory criteria (100-104 west of the Blue Ridge, \n105-109 east of the Blue Ridge) can't be ruled out, but the more \nlikely scenario is for heat indices a bit below those values.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Thunderstorm chances increase next week.\n\nDisturbances rounding a ridge to the south and west in NW flow aloft \nwill introduce shower and thunderstorm chances at times next week. \nThe timing and amplitude of these waves have very low predictability \nbeyond a couple days in advance (at best). But the general theme \nwill be enhanced lift and shear clashing with an increasingly hot \nand unstable airmass over or near the Mid-Atlantic. AI/ML guidance \nhas broad elevated probabilities of severe weather much of next \nweek, the details (including timing and magnitude/potential impact) \nof which will be ironed out in the coming days.\n\nIt should be noted that there is a conditional potential for one or \nmore organized clusters of strong to severe storms at some point \nnext week. The most likely time currently appears to be around mid \nweek, but changes are to be expected at this time range.",
